{%- comment -%}
  sections/lada-gateway.liquid — the whole of /pages/lada.

  WHAT THIS REPLACES. The Lada page was nine sections: hero, proof
  seals, a filtered grid, a three-stage craft story, a snapping
  lookbook, a measuring service, a sizing table, a guarantee and a
  house card. The client read it and said the same thing three times:
  too much writing, too many choices, words a customer would not use.
  He is right. A man who has never heard of Lada cannot be asked to
  read eight hundred words before he is allowed to see a waistcoat.

  So the page is now one screen with two doors on it, and nothing else.
  The reference the client gave is faizasaqlain.pk, where the entire
  copy above the grid is the single word "Bridals" and the photographs
  do all the remaining work. Everything under Lada is cotton and there
  are exactly two ways in:

    Alamgeer       100% combed cotton, cut to the customer's measurement
    Ready to wear  stitched waistcoats in S, M, L

  DO NOT ADD A KICKER, A LEDE, AN INTRO, A SCROLL CUE OR A SEAL ROW
  BACK TO THIS FILE. Every one of those was here before and every one
  of them is why the page was rejected. If a thing needs a paragraph to
  justify itself on this screen, it belongs on the collection page
  behind the door, not in front of it.

  THE BREADCRUMB IS NOT COPY AND IS NOT ONE OF THOSE. It is the same
  <nav class="crumb"> every other page on this store opens with, and it
  is here because its absence was a large part of why Lada read as a
  different website rather than a room inside this one. Two links and a
  slash, in the dark register. Leave it.

  THE DOORS MUST NEVER DISAPPEAR. /collections/alamgeer and
  /collections/ready-to-wear both exist now, as smart collections on a
  tag rule, so both hrefs resolve. The hand-built fallback below stays
  anyway: an earlier pattern in this theme hides a link when the
  collection is missing, which here would leave a visitor staring at
  half a screen, and the client can rename a collection in admin at any
  time. So the handle is a setting, the URL is composed by hand when the
  collection is not found, and the door renders either way.
  Wrong-but-present beats absent.

  NO PHOTOGRAPHS IS THE SHIPPING STATE, not an error state. The client
  shoots these later. With nothing picked a door is a --lada-panel
  plate with a champagne hairline and its name on it, which is a
  dignified thing to look at, not a grey box with a torn-image glyph.
  That is also why the asset-filename fallbacks default to empty rather
  than to some other product's picture: a borrowed photograph of a
  waistcoat on the Alamgeer door would be a lie, and lies are worse
  than blanks. men-unstitched.webp and lada-waistcoats.webp are already
  in assets/ if he wants a stand-in before the shoot.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign crest_label = section.settings.mark_label | default: 'Lada'
-%}

<section class="lada lada-gate" aria-labelledby="lada-gate-h">

  {%- comment -%}
    THE BREADCRUMB. Same element, same classes and same separator as
    sections/main-collection.liquid, so one rule set dresses both and
    the two halves of the store cannot drift apart again. .crumb--lada
    is the only addition: the shared rule paints --text-tertiary, which
    is a dark grey and disappears on a dark ground, so the modifier is
    the hook the Lada palette hangs off. Everything else about the
    element (the flex row, the 12px tap targets on a phone, the
    aria-current on the last item) comes free from .crumb.

    Home is the only real link: this IS the Lada page, so the second
    item is the current page and is marked as such rather than linking
    to itself.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <nav class="crumb crumb--lada lada-gate__crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="{{ routes.root_url }}">Home</a><i>/</i>
    <span aria-current="page">{{ page.title | default: crest_label }}</span>
  </nav>

  {%- comment -%}
    ONE h1 ON THE PAGE, ALWAYS, AND IT IS THE LABEL'S NAME.

    The mark is optional because the header already says Lada, so on a
    phone the client may want the doors to take the whole screen. But
    the heading cannot be optional with it: a page whose only headings
    are two door names has no top-level heading at all, and a screen
    reader user landing here gets two h2s and no idea where he is. So
    the checkbox switches the mark between drawn and .vh, never between
    present and absent. .vh is site.css's clip utility.

    The section is named by this heading with aria-labelledby rather
    than by a repeated aria-label, which is how lada-band already does
    it: one string, edited in one place, and no chance of the landmark
    and the heading drifting apart.

    The mark is the client's own artwork on a transparent ground, which
    is why it is an <img> and not type — see snippets/kj-mark.liquid.
    It is the first paint on its own page, so it loads eagerly, and
    sizes restates the crest's own clamp() so a phone takes the 260px
    file instead of downloading 600px of artwork to paint 84.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if section.settings.show_mark -%}
    <h1 class="lada-gate__crest" id="lada-gate-h">
      {%- render 'kj-mark', mark: 'lada', label: crest_label, loading: 'eager',
                 sizes: '(min-width:1240px) 124px, 10vw' -%}
    </h1>
  {%- else -%}
    <h1 class="lada-gate__crest vh" id="lada-gate-h">{{ crest_label }}</h1>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    A list of two, so the doors are announced as "list, 2 items" and a
    visitor knows the choice is finished before he starts reading it.
    role="list" because list-style:none drops list semantics in Safari.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <ul class="lada-gate__doors" role="list">
    {%- for i in (1..2) -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        ONLY THE NAME IS DEFAULTED HERE, AND THAT IS ON PURPOSE.

        `| default:` fires on an empty string as well as on nil, so a
        setting that carries one can never be emptied: the client
        deletes the line in the editor, saves, and the old words come
        straight back. On the note that is the wrong behaviour twice
        over. The complaint that produced this page was too much
        writing, so deleting a line has to be a thing he can do; and
        the `{% if note != blank %}` below could never be false, which
        is dead code pretending to be a guard. The schema default still
        writes both lines when the section is first added, so nothing
        changes out of the box — only the delete key starts working.
        Same for the handle: with a default here the blank-handle
        branch under this comment was unreachable.

        The name keeps its default, because a door with no name and an
        empty alt on its photograph is a link with no accessible name
        at all. That one is worth forcing.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- liquid
        case i
          when 1
            assign pic    = section.settings.door1_image
            assign file   = section.settings.door1_asset
            assign name   = section.settings.door1_name | default: 'Alamgeer'
            assign note   = section.settings.door1_note
            assign handle = section.settings.door1_handle
          when 2
            assign pic    = section.settings.door2_image
            assign file   = section.settings.door2_asset
            assign name   = section.settings.door2_name | default: 'Ready to wear'
            assign note   = section.settings.door2_note
            assign handle = section.settings.door2_handle
        endcase

        # collections[handle] on a handle that does not exist yet returns an
        # empty drop whose .url is blank, and href="" reloads the current
        # page — which is the one failure mode a gateway cannot have. So the
        # path is composed from routes.collections_url when the collection is
        # missing. The instant the client creates it, the first branch takes
        # over and the link starts resolving with no edit here.
        assign target = collections[handle]
        if target.id
          assign href = target.url
        elsif handle != blank
          assign href = routes.collections_url | append: '/' | append: handle
        else
          assign href = routes.all_products_collection_url
        endif

        # Never ask Shopify for a width the file does not have: asking 1600
        # of a 900px upload returns a 1.8x upscale that can only look soft.
        # Candidates are emitted only up to the file's own width, capped at
        # 2000 so a 6000px camera original does not get served to a laptop.
        assign cap = 0
        if pic != blank
          assign cap = pic.width
          if cap > 2000
            assign cap = 2000
          endif
        endif
      -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        data-reveal is the site's own scroll-reveal hook, the one
        sections/category-grid.liquid and sections/store-location.liquid
        already use, and --d is the delay it reads. The client asked for
        smooth animations that are CONSISTENT, so the Lada journey opts
        into the effect the white shop already has rather than growing a
        second one of its own. 90ms between the two doors is enough to
        read as one movement with a follow rather than as two.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <li class="lada-gate__cell" data-reveal style="--d:{{ i | minus: 1 | times: 90 }}ms">
        <a class="lada-gate__door{% if pic == blank and file == blank %} lada-gate__door--bare{% endif %}"
           href="{{ href }}">

          {%- comment -%}
            alt is empty on purpose. The door's accessible name is the two
            lines of text inside the same anchor, and a described
            photograph would be read out on top of them, so the same link
            would announce twice. Decorative is the correct call whenever
            readable text sits in the link with the image.

            Both doors load eagerly: side by side on desktop they are both
            in the first screen, and lazy-loading the right-hand one would
            leave half the page blank on arrival.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- if pic != blank -%}
            <span class="lada-gate__media">
              <img src="{{ pic | image_url: width: cap }}"
                   srcset="{%- if cap > 700 %}{{ pic | image_url: width: 700 }} 700w,{% endif -%}
                           {%- if cap > 1100 %}{{ pic | image_url: width: 1100 }} 1100w,{% endif -%}
                           {%- if cap > 1500 %}{{ pic | image_url: width: 1500 }} 1500w,{% endif -%}
                           {{ pic | image_url: width: cap }} {{ cap }}w"
                   sizes="(min-width:600px) 50vw, 100vw"
                   width="{{ pic.width }}" height="{{ pic.height }}"
                   loading="eager"{% if i == 1 %} fetchpriority="high"{% endif %} decoding="async" alt="">
            </span>
          {%- elsif file != blank -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              A TYPED FILENAME HAS NO DIMENSIONS LIQUID CAN READ, so the
              pair below is declared rather than measured, and 1000x1250
              is the size of both stand-ins actually shipped in assets/
              (men-unstitched.webp and lada-waistcoats.webp), which is
              what the schema tells the client to type here.

              It is safe even when he types something else. The image is
              laid out entirely by the door: .lada-gate__media is
              inset:0 on a cell with its own min-block-size, and the img
              is width:100%/height:100%/object-fit:cover inside it, so
              the declared ratio never sizes anything on the page and a
              wrong pair cannot crop, stretch or shift a door. What the
              pair does buy is the browser's intrinsic-ratio reservation
              before the bytes land, which is the same thing the picked
              image branch above gets from pic.width / pic.height, and
              it clears ImgWidthAndHeight, the one real theme-check
              error in this theme's own code.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <span class="lada-gate__media">
              <img src="{{ file | asset_url }}" width="1000" height="1250"
                   loading="eager"{% if i == 1 %} fetchpriority="high"{% endif %} decoding="async" alt="">
            </span>
          {%- endif -%}

          <span class="lada-gate__plate">
            <span class="lada-gate__name">{{ name }}</span>
            {%- if note != blank -%}
              <span class="lada-gate__note">{{ note }}</span>
            {%- endif -%}
          </span>
        </a>
      </li>
    {%- endfor -%}
  </ul>
</section>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Lada gateway",
  "settings": [
    { "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The whole Lada page. Two doors, nothing else. Please do not add a heading, an intro or a strapline above them. The photographs and the two names are the design." },

    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "show_mark", "label": "Show the Lada mark above the doors", "default": true },
    { "type": "text", "id": "mark_label", "label": "Page name", "default": "Lada",
      "info": "Read out by screen readers, and used as the page heading when the mark is hidden. Type it in normal case; the uppercase on the doors is added by the design." },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Left door" },
    { "type": "image_picker", "id": "door1_image", "label": "Photograph" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "door1_asset", "label": "Or theme asset filename",
      "info": "Used only when no photograph is picked. men-unstitched.webp is already in the theme if you want a stand-in. Leave empty and the door is a plain panel, which is fine." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "door1_name", "label": "Name", "default": "Alamgeer" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "door1_note", "label": "What it is", "default": "100% combed cotton, cut to your size",
      "info": "A few plain words. Keep it short, or clear the box and the door shows the name on its own." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "door1_handle", "label": "Collection handle", "default": "alamgeer",
      "info": "The end of the web address, for example alamgeer for /collections/alamgeer. The door works the moment you create that collection. Clear the box and it opens all products instead." },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Right door" },
    { "type": "image_picker", "id": "door2_image", "label": "Photograph" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "door2_asset", "label": "Or theme asset filename",
      "info": "Used only when no photograph is picked. lada-waistcoats.webp is already in the theme if you want a stand-in." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "door2_name", "label": "Name", "default": "Ready to wear" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "door2_note", "label": "What it is", "default": "Stitched waistcoats, S to L",
      "info": "Clear the box and the door shows the name on its own." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "door2_handle", "label": "Collection handle", "default": "ready-to-wear",
      "info": "The end of the web address, for example ready-to-wear for /collections/ready-to-wear. Clear the box and it opens all products instead." }
  ],
  "presets": [{ "name": "Lada gateway" }]
}
{% endschema %}
